## Why This Is Asked
Streaming data often requires maintaining a running statistic under insertions and deletions. The median is robust and a common interview target for dynamic data structures.

## Key Concepts
- Two-heap approach: keep lower half in a max-heap and upper half in a min-heap.
- Duplicates supported; lazy deletion via a delay map.
- Rebalancing invariant: size difference at most 1; median derived from heap tops.

## Code Example
```javascript
class MedianDS {
  constructor(){
    this.lo = new MaxHeap(); // lower half
    this.hi = new MinHeap(); // upper half
    this.delayed = new Map();
    this.sizeLo = 0;
    this.sizeHi = 0;
  }
  _cleanTop(h) {
    const top = h.peek();
    while (top !== null && this.delayed.get(top) > 0) {
      h.pop();
      this.delayed.set(top, this.delayed.get(top) - 1);
    }
  }
  add(x){
    if (this.sizeLo === 0 || x <= this.lo.peek()) {
      this.lo.push(x); this.sizeLo++;
    } else { this.hi.push(x); this.sizeHi++; }
    this._balance();
  }
  remove(x){
    const loTop = this.lo.peek();
    if (loTop !== null && x <= loTop) {
      this.delayed.set(x, (this.delayed.get(x) || 0) + 1);
      this.sizeLo--;
      if (x === loTop) this._cleanTop(this.lo);
    } else {
      this.delayed.set(x, (this.delayed.get(x) || 0) + 1);
      this.sizeHi--;
      this._cleanTop(this.hi);
    }
    this._balance();
  }
  _balance(){
    if (this.sizeLo > this.sizeHi + 1) {
      this.hi.push(this.lo.pop()); this.sizeLo--; this.sizeHi++;
    } else if (this.sizeHi > this.sizeLo) {
      this.lo.push(this.hi.pop()); this.sizeHi--; this.sizeLo++;
    }
    this._cleanTop(this.lo); this._cleanTop(this.hi);
  }
  getMedian(){
    if (this.sizeLo === 0) return null;
    if (this.sizeLo > this.sizeHi) return this.lo.peek();
    return (this.lo.peek() + this.hi.peek()) / 2;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Dynamic string editing with fast substring queries is critical in modern editors and versioned docs. A rope with an implicit treap provides randomized balance and localized updates, enabling logarithmic edits while maintaining per-substring hashes for quick comparisons.

## Key Concepts

- Implicit treap (rope) structure for sequence storage
- Subtree size and rolling hash maintenance
- Polynomial rolling hash with two moduli to reduce collisions
- Split and merge operations for positional edits
- Substring hash isolation without copying strings

## Code Example

```javascript
class Node {
  constructor(ch) {
    this.c = ch;
    this.pr = Math.random();
    this.left = null; this.right = null;
    this.sz = 1;
    this.h1 = ch.charCodeAt(0);
    this.h2 = ch.charCodeAt(0);
  }
}
// Update size and hashes from children
function upd(n){ if(!n) return; n.sz = 1; n.h1 = n.c.charCodeAt(0); n.h2 = n.c.charCodeAt(0);
  if(n.left){ n.sz += n.left.sz; n.h1 = n.left.h1 + somePow1; n.h2 = n.left.h2 + somePow2; }
  if(n.right){ n.sz += n.right.sz; n.h1 = n.h1 + rightHash1; n.h2 = n.h2 + rightHash2; }
}
function split(t, k){ // split first k chars to a
  if(!t) return [null, null];
  if(size(t.left) >= k){ const [l, r] = split(t.left, k); t.left = r; upd(t); return [l, t]; }
  const [l, r] = split(t.right, k - size(t.left) - 1); t.right = l; upd(t); return [t, r];
}
function merge(a, b){ if(!a||!b) return a||b; if(a.pr < b.pr){ a.right = merge(a.right, b); upd(a); return a; } else { b.left = merge(a, b.left); upd(b); return b; } }
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend for Unicode normalization? 
- How to handle concurrent edits in a collaborative editor? 
- Can you quantify memory overhead and compare with a pure block-based rope?
